===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|swellen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swellan||to swell}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*swellan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swellaną|t=to swell}}, of unknown origin.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|swälle|t=to swell}}, {{cog|fy|swolle|t=to swell}}, {{cog|nl|zwellen|t=to swell}}, {{cog|nds|swellen|t=to swell}}, {{cog|de|schwellen|t=to swell}}, {{cog|sv|svälla|t=to swell}}, {{cog|is|svella}}. The adjective may derive from the noun.<!--with the sense development being from 'arrogance or pomp' (a common metaphor in many languages including Irish and Spanish and found in English in the 1730s), then to a meaning of a posh or fancy person (found in English by the 1780s), thus to the use of the adjective to mean "well-dressed, rich" (found in English by the 1810s) and thus to the general meaning of high class or excellent.-->

====Verb====
{{en-verb||||past2=swole|past3=swoll|swollen|past_ptc2=swelled}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become bigger, especially due to being [[engorge]]d.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|prologue|text=O for a Muse of fire, that would ascend<br>The brightest heaven of invention,<br>A kingdom for a stage, princes to act<br>And monarchs to behold the '''swelling''' scene!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=w:P. C. Wren|title=Snake and Sword|location=London|publisher=Longmans, Green|chapter=5|page=78|url=https://archive.org/stream/snakeswordnovel00wrenrich#page/78/mode/1up
|passage=“If you drinks a drop more, Miss Lucy, you’ll just go like my pore young sister goed, {{...}} Pop she did not. She '''swole'''&nbsp;… '''swole''' and '''swole'''.”<br>“You mean ‘'''swelled''',’ Cookoo,” corrected Lucille {{...}}<br>“{{...}} ''I'' say she '''swole'''—and what is more she '''swole''' clean into a dropsy.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Stephen King|title=The Girl Who Loved Tom Gordon: A Novel|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Scribner]]|date=6 April 1999|isbn=978-0-684-86762-5|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=w:Pocket Books|year2=May 2017|pages2=67–68|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=8MTqKDAsgpwC&pg=PA68|isbn2=978-1-5011-5751-6|passage=She had overheard her Mom and Mrs. Thomas from across the street talking about someone who was allergic to stings, and Mrs. Thomas had said, "Ten seconds after it gut im, poor ole Frank was '''swole''' up like a balloon. If he hadn't had his little kit with the hyperdermic, I guess he woulda choked to death."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to become bigger.
#: {{ux|en|Rains and dissolving snow '''swell''' the rivers in spring.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1633|author=w:John Donne|chapter=The Storme|title=Poems|location=London|publisher=John Marriot|page=57|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A69225.0001.001
|passage=Mildly it [the wind] kist our sailes, and, fresh, and sweet,<br>As, to a stomack [[starve|sterv’d]], whose insides meete,<br>Meate comes, it came; and '''swole''' our sailes, when wee<br>So joyd, as ''{{w|Sarah|Sara’}}'' her swelling joy’d to see.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1687|author=w:Francis Atterbury|title=An Answer to Some Considerations on the Spirit of Martin Luther and the Original of the Reformation|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A26149.0001.001|page=12|publisher=Oxford
|passage=’Tis low ebb sure with his Accuser, when such Peccadillos as these are put in to '''swell''' the Charge.}}
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Affair at the Novelty Theatre|passage=For this scene, a large number of supers are engaged, and in order to further '''swell''' the crowd, practically all the available stage hands have to ‘walk on’ dressed in various coloured dominoes, and all wearing masks.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 18 2013|author=w:Simon Romero|title=Protests Widen as Brazilians Chide Leaders|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/19/world/americas/brazilian-leaders-brace-for-more-protests.html|journal=New York Times|accessdate=21 June 2013
|passage=After a harsh police crackdown last week fueled anger and '''swelled''' protests, President Dilma Rousseff, a former guerrilla who was imprisoned under the dictatorship and has now become the target of pointed criticism herself, tried to appease dissenters by embracing their cause on Tuesday.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To grow gradually in force or loudness.
#: {{ux|en|The organ music '''swelled'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to grow gradually in force or loudness.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1880|author=Felix Leopold Oswald|title=Summerland Sketches|page=57
|passage=It commenced with a slow crescendo, so irresistibly lugubrious that two of our dogs at once raised their heads and '''swelled''' their voices into a responsive tremolo, which may have been heard and appreciated by their distant relatives.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To raise to arrogance; to puff up; to inflate.
#: {{ux|en|to be '''swelled''' with pride or haughtiness}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be raised to [[arrogance]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|5|1|text=Why, here he comes, '''swelling''' like a turkey-cock.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|volume=3|chapter=9|page=111|text={{...}} you '''swell''' at the sight of tartan, as the bull is said to do at scarlet.}}
# To be [[elated]]; to rise arrogantly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1662|author=w:John Dryden|title=To My Lord Chancellor Presented on New-Years-Day|location=London|publisher=Henry Herringman|page=5|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A36703.0001.001
|passage=In all things else above our humble fate<br>Your equal mind yet '''swells''' not into state,<br>But like some mountain in those happy Isles<br>Where in perpetual Spring young Nature smiles,<br>Your greatnesse shows:}}
# To be turgid, bombastic, or extravagant.
#: {{ux|en|'''swelling''' words&emsp; a '''swelling''' style}}
# To [[protuberate]]; to [[bulge]] out.
#: {{ux|en|A cask '''swells''' in the middle.}}

