===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|swelle}}, from the verb {{m|enm|swellen}} (modern {{m|en|swell}}).

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of swelling; increase in size.
# A [[bulge]] or [[protuberance]].
# Increase of power in style, or of rhetorical force.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1826|author=w:Walter Savage Landor|title=w:Imaginary Conversations|location=London|publisher=Henry Colburn|edition=2nd|section=Volume I, Conversation 6, p. 128|url=https://archive.org/details/imaginaryconver05landgoog
|passage=Concentrated are his arguments, select and distinct and orderly his topics, ready and unfastidious his expressions, popular his allusions, plain his illustrations, easy the '''swell''' and subsidence of his periods {{...}}}}
# A long series of [[ocean]] [[wave]]s, generally produced by [[wind]], and lasting after the wind has ceased.
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|text=There was a great, smooth '''swell''' upon the sea.|chapter=24}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 February 8|author=Barry Doe|title=Birmingham & West Midlands Atlas is a fine production|journal=RAIL|issue=976|page=63,  photo caption|text=The Tilbury-Gravesend foot passenger ferry fights its way through a high '''swell''' on the river Thames on February 16 2022.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A gradual [[crescendo]] followed by [[diminuendo]].
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=5|passage=He was thinking; but the glory of the song, the '''swell''' from the great organ, the clustered lights, {{...}}, the height and vastness of this noble fane, its antiquity and its strength—all these things seemed to have their part as causes of the thrilling emotion that accompanied his thoughts.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A device for controlling the [[volume]] of a [[pipe organ]].
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[division]] in a [[pipe organ]], usually the largest [[enclosed]] division.
# A [[hillock]] or similar raised area of [[terrain]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1909|author=w:Joseph A. Altsheler|title=The Last of the Chiefs|chapter=2
|passage=Off on the crest of a '''swell''' a moving figure was seen now and then. "Antelope," said the hunters.}}
# {{lb|en|geology}} An [[upward]] [[protrusion]] of [[stratum|strata]] from whose central region the [[bed]]s dip [[quaquaversal]]ly at a low angle.
# {{lb|en|informal|dated}} A person who is [[stylish]], [[fancy]], or [[elegant]].
#: {{syn|en|dandy|dude|toff|Thesaurus:dandy}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1850|author=w:William Makepeace Thackeray|chapter=The Kickleburys on the Rhine|title=The Christmas Books of Mr. M. A. Titmarsh
|passage=It costs him no more to wear all his ornaments about his distinguished person than to leave them at home. If you can be a '''swell''' at a cheap rate, why not?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1887|author=w:Horatio Alger|title=The Cash Boy|chapter=9
|passage=He was dressed in a flashy style, not unlike what is popularly denominated a '''swell'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892| title=Occident - Volume 22 |author=| page=36| passage=Between the two extremes of college men the unsocial dig and the flunking '''swell''', lies the majority, who, acknowledging the duty and merit of hard work, see the value in social and recreative line, but are at somewhat of a loss, seemingly, how to proportionize the time given to the different sides of college life, or how far to allow themselves to go on the more attractive side.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A person of high [[social]] [[standing]]; an important person.
#: {{syn|en|toff}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1864|author=w:Anthony Trollope|title=The Small House at Allington|chapter=2
|passage="I am not in Mr Crosbie's confidence. He is in the General Committee Office, I know; and, I believe, has pretty nearly the management of the whole of it."<br>"I'll tell you what he is, Bell; Mr Crosbie is a '''swell'''." And Lilian Dale was right; Mr Crosbie was a '''swell'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1900|author=w:Joseph Conrad|title=w:Lord Jim|location=Edinburgh and London|publisher=William Blackwood|chapter=14|page=176|url=https://archive.org/details/lordjimtale00conrrich/page/176
|passage=The only sensible man I came across was the cabman who drove me about. A broken-down '''swell''' he was, I fancy.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1906|author=w:Gilbert Parker|title=The Trespasser|chapter=8
|passage=You buy a lot of Indian or halfbreed loafers with beaver-skins and rum, go to the Mount of the Burning Arrows, and these fellows dance round you and call you one of the lost race, the Mighty Men of the Kimash Hills. And they'll do that while the rum lasts. Meanwhile you get to think yourself a devil of a '''swell'''—you and the gods!}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author=w:Graham Greene|title={{w|Brighton Rock (novel)|Brighton Rock}}|location=New York|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2002|section=Part Seven, Chapter 3, p. 209|url=https://archive.org/details/brightonrockvint00gree
|passage=‘{{...}} Colleoni’s going to take over this place from you, and he’s got his lawyer. A man in London. A '''swell'''.’}}
# {{C|en|Horse tack}} The [[front]] [[brow]] of a [[saddle bow]], connected in the [[tree]] by the two [[saddle bar]]s to the [[cantle]] on the other end.
#: {{syn|en|pommel|fork}}

